#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""conformance -- /standard's twelve items, measured (proposal 21, S-04).

  conformance [--project DIR] [--json]

The sponsor made the standard mandatory for every project (proposal 21,
A-01 to A-04), and a session reports "standard: N of 12 hold". That number
comes from here, not from a session's say-so. The contract is
skills/standard/SKILL.md, sections 2 and 3; each item below is the
mechanical rule this tool applies for it.

OUTPUT. The first line is `standard: N of 12 hold`, then one line per item:
`✓` holds, `✗` does not hold (why, then the fix), `…` waiting on
common-rules (what RULES is missing). Every value printed -- in the text and
in --json -- goes through bin/warmup's shown(), so a ledger id or a path
cannot forge a line or hide text. --json gives
{"project", "hold", "total": 12, "items": [{"n", "name", "state", "why", "fix"}]},
where state is "holds", "does not hold" or "waiting".

DIR is resolved to its git top level, as bin/warmup does.

THE TWELVE RULES

 1 Rules read and implemented. Holds when DIR is the rules repository
   itself: the same checkout as this tool, or a checkout sharing its git
   common directory (a worktree of it). rulecheck reports that case as
   "nothing to align against". Otherwise `.common-rules-version` must exist,
   be tracked (`git ls-files --error-unmatch`), and on disk be exactly the
   blob at HEAD: an uncommitted stamp is no record. Then bin/rulecheck's own
   mandatory_pending(), called in-process, decides by its state:
     aligned                          holds
     ahead                            holds, "ahead of this rules checkout --
                                      update common-rules"; never the project's fault
     behind, no entry pending         holds (information only)
     behind, entries (diverged too)   does not hold, naming the headings
     unresolvable                     does not hold: "the stamp names no commit in
                                      common-rules", listing every entry it counts
     could not check, not adopting,
     anything else                    does not hold, saying which
   Without mandatory_pending() in RULES the item waits on common-rules.
 2 Migrated. tools/tracker/ledger.find() finds at least one ledger. Every
   ledger found parses and validate()s. The project's one tracker page,
   docs/proposals/tracker/index.html, is fresh: tools/tracker/board.
   project_page_state(), the digests `tracker board --check` compares
   (proposal 22, T-02). A ledger that records its own tracker (`tracker.own`)
   has a fresh page of its own too: render.freshness(), what `tracker render
   --check` calls. No other ledger's per-proposal page is read.
 3 Declared. `.common-rules.json` exists, tools/project.problems() is empty,
   and tools/project.declared() -- only what the file validly declares, no
   defaults and no `.common-rules-test` fallback -- has read_order,
   safety_rules, gates.quick and gates.merge. plan_check and plan_page are
   asked for only "when the project has its own generator", which no tool
   can see, so they are not checked.
 4 Installed. All of these:
   - the git pre-commit hook (`git rev-parse --git-path hooks`, which
     honours core.hooksPath) is byte for byte what this derecord installs:
     the `<<'HOOKBODY'` template read from bin/derecord, with the hook's own
     RULES="..." substituted, under the `# derecord-body-sha256:` line
     derecord computes -- and that RULES must be a rules checkout (below). A
     self-consistent sha line alone is only derecord's "may I overwrite"
     test: a forged hook, a hand edit, a hook from an older derecord or one
     naming a copy of the rules does not hold. The fix is to re-run derecord;
   - an adopter's `.claude/settings.json` has a SessionStart, a PreCompact and
     a Stop command for hooks/sessionstart, hooks/precompact and hooks/stop,
     and a PostToolUse command for hooks/posttooluse-agent. Each command,
     resolved through symlinks, must be RULES_DIR/hooks/<name>, or hooks/<name>
     at the top of a checkout sharing RULES_DIR's git common directory (the
     main checkout, or a worktree of it). A copied hooks/ directory does not
     hold. The matcher is not checked; derecord corrects it. The Common Rules
     plugin repository itself uses its portable hooks/hooks.json instead of
     keeping a second, machine-specific .claude/settings.json copy;
   - every line in bin/derecord's IGNORE_LINES (read from derecord, so the
     two cannot drift) is decided by git, never by a verbatim line, which a
     later `!line` undoes: `git check-ignore -v -z --stdin --no-index` shows a `.gitignore` file inside
     the project ignoring its path, as written or as a directory, with a
     pattern that is not a negation. The PhotoVault app writes `.swarm/*`
     at the top and ignores `.claude-flow/data` from its own
     `.claude-flow/.gitignore`: the same paths, other spellings.
     `.git/info/exclude` and a personal core.excludesFile do not count,
     even one named .gitignore, because a clone would track the file;
   - `git ls-files -- <line>` lists nothing for any of those lines, the
     same pathspec derecord uses to warn.
 5 Lead prompt. The prompts are every regular *lead-prompt*.md file at most
   three levels under docs/ (derecord's `find docs -maxdepth 3`, which
   covers docs/handovers/), not under a `superseded` folder. There must be
   at least one. When RULES' templates/lead-prompt.md does not carry
   `<!-- common-rules:lead-prompt proposal/21 -->` the item is waiting on
   common-rules (S-06). Otherwise every prompt must carry that marker.
   A PROXY: the marker shows a prompt was regenerated from the template, not
   that it carries the project's own rules or says anything in particular.
 6 The ledger speaks the common language. For every ledger:
   - every blocked row, and every open ask of kind decision, has an `owner`
     matching tools/tracker/ledger.py's OWNER: sponsor, lead or
     session:<name> ("bob" does not hold);
   - every row's `tag` is `[ruflo · <tier> · <model>]`, and exactly
     f"[ruflo · {tier} · {model}]" when the row has tier and model fields;
   - exactly one of the keys `tiers` and `model_routing` is present;
   - the problems validate() finds with `requests` are none: validate()
     of the ledger minus validate() of the ledger without `requests`.
     A PROXY for requests: their shape is checked, but whether the other
     session named in `from`/`to` exists or knows of the request cannot be
     verified read-only from this project.
   Offending ids are named, the first five of each kind. No ledger does not
   hold.
 7 Proposals. `bin/proposalcheck --project DIR` exits 0. Exit 2, no
   docs/proposals, does not hold.
 8 No hand-kept duplicates. Holds when .common-rules.json validly declares
   plan_page. Otherwise, for every ledger NN-slug.json, a sibling
   NN-slug.md either does not exist, or has at most 5 lines and contains
   the ledger's file name. No ledger: nothing to duplicate, so it holds.
   A PROXY: only a same-stem .md twin is checked. A hand-kept mirror under
   any other name, or a hand-updated artifact, is not seen.
 9 Ruflo around items (RF-01: evidence, not a folder). Checked in order:
   - no ledger under docs/proposals: does not hold -- there is nothing to
     check Ruflo evidence against;
   - a ledger records switches.ruflo off: holds (reported as "switched off
     by ...");
   - .swarm/memory.db (DIR's own, else its main checkout's -- the database
     lives only there, never in a worktree) is absent, 0 bytes, or not a
     readable sqlite database with a memory_entries table: does not hold,
     naming why;
   - otherwise: every item, across every ledger, whose status is "done" and
     whose own close date -- the last log entry's own `status` key equal to
     "done" (what `tracker set --status done` and `apply-staged` write,
     independently of `--event`'s free text), else, for older rows with no
     `status`-keyed entry, the last entry whose `event` is exactly "done"
     (case-insensitive) -- is dated on or after 2026-09-17 -- the date this
     item's own CHANGELOG entry lands, so history before the fix is reported,
     never failed -- must have both `item:<ID>:start` and `item:<ID>:done`
     among memory_entries's keys, read read-only (`file:...?mode=ro`) under
     the project's resolved namespace (tools/ruflo.py). Missing either key
     for any such id: does not hold, naming every missing id (first five). An
     id shared by two ledgers is satisfied by one record for both -- there is
     only one Ruflo memory, keyed by id alone. A done item with no dated
     close entry of either kind is counted as before the cutoff, never
     required. Holds
     otherwise, and the why names how many pre-cutoff items were not counted.
   Ruflo memory is never read through its CLI, which auto-starts a daemon --
   only straight sqlite3, read-only.
10 Publishing recorded. Every docs/proposals/tracker/*.published.json is
   well-formed. index.published.json, the project page's record (proposal 22,
   T-03), is read by bin/warmup's own project_publish_state(). A per-ledger
   record beside its ledger is read by bin/warmup's publish_state(), but only
   for a ledger that records its own tracker (`tracker.own`) or where
   .common-rules.json declares plan_page; a leftover per-ledger sidecar of any
   other ledger does not hold -- nothing renders or publishes that page any
   more -- and the fix is to `git rm` it and record the project page with
   `tracker published --project`. A sidecar with no ledger at all is read with
   tools/tracker/render.published_problems(). None is read through a symlink
   or from outside DIR. "Page changed since publish" is a to-do, not a
   failure. No sidecar holds.
11 CLAUDE.md pointer. A CLAUDE.md that is a symlink, or resolves outside the
   project, is not read and does not hold -- the policy of items 5 and 10.
   CLAUDE.md contains the `<!-- common-rules:warmup -->`
   ... `<!-- /common-rules:warmup -->` block, and the block equals
   tools/migrate.pointer_block() for the read order tools/project.load()
   gives. CLAUDE.md must be tracked at HEAD, and when
   `git diff HEAD -- CLAUDE.md` is not empty, the block at HEAD must equal
   the block on disk. That is: the uncommitted diff does not touch the
   block. Staged but uncommitted counts as uncommitted. The diff runs as its
   plumbing form, `git diff-index -p HEAD`, because porcelain `git diff`
   rewrites .git/index when the stat data is stale.
12 Card. `bin/warmup --project DIR --check --no-recall` exits 0.

READ-ONLY. Nothing here writes. It runs two tools, each confirmed read-only
from its code -- `proposalcheck`, and `warmup --check --no-recall` with no
--state or --migrate -- and calls rulecheck's mandatory_pending() in-process,
which only reads. Every git and child process runs with GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0,
and the tool sets it in its own environment before calling rulecheck
in-process, whose git runs inherit it; so a `git diff` or a warmup `git log`
never refreshes an index opportunistically. Children also get
PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1. This tool's own git calls add
`-c core.fsmonitor=false`, `--no-ext-diff` and `--no-textconv`: a project's
config or .gitattributes must not make a read run a command.
tests/test_conformance.py snapshots every byte and mtime of a project,
.git included, around a run.

Exit codes:
  0  no item does not hold. A waiting item does not make it 1: what is
     missing is in common-rules, not the project.
  1  any item does not hold.
  2  could not run: DIR is not inside a git repository, or bin/warmup
     could not be loaded.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import contextlib
import datetime
import functools
import hashlib
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import io
import json
import os
import re
import sqlite3
import subprocess
import sys
import threading
import traceback
from concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor
from pathlib import Path, PurePosixPath
from typing import NamedTuple

sys.dont_write_bytecode = True
RULES_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent
sys.path.insert(0, str(RULES_DIR))

from tools import migrate as M  # noqa: E402
from tools import project as P  # noqa: E402
from tools import ruflo as RU  # noqa: E402
from tools.tracker import board as B  # noqa: E402
from tools.tracker import ledger as L  # noqa: E402
from tools.tracker import render as R  # noqa: E402

HOLDS, NOT, WAITING = "holds", "does not hold", "waiting"
TOTAL = 12
TIMEOUT = 900
STAMP = ".common-rules-version"
LEAD_TEMPLATE = RULES_DIR / "templates" / "lead-prompt.md"
LEAD_MARKER = "<!-- common-rules:lead-prompt proposal/21 -->"
SETTINGS_HOOKS = (("SessionStart", "sessionstart"), ("PreCompact", "precompact"),
                  ("Stop", "stop"), ("PostToolUse", "posttooluse-agent"))
FIRST = 5
_TAG_PART = r"[^·\[\]\s](?:[^·\[\]\x00-\x1f]*[^·\[\]\s])?"
TAG = re.compile(rf"\[ruflo · ({_TAG_PART}) · ({_TAG_PART})\]")
RUFLO_CUTOFF = datetime.date(2026, 9, 17)  # RF-01: this item's own CHANGELOG entry date


def _load(name: str, path: Path):
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader(name, str(path))
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader(loader.name, loader)
    module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    # Registered under its private name before exec: a bare exec_module()
    # loads fine for a module with no @dataclass, but @dataclass's own
    # _is_type looks its module up via sys.modules[cls.__module__] -- absent
    # here (the module is loaded by path, not by import), that lookup raises
    # instead of returning None. bin/proposalcheck (O-09) hit exactly this.
    sys.modules[name] = module
    try:
        loader.exec_module(module)
    except BaseException:
        sys.modules.pop(name, None)
        raise
    return module


try:
    W = _load("_conformance_warmup", RULES_DIR / "bin" / "warmup")
except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 -- any failure to load is "could not run", reported by main()
    W = None

try:
    PC = _load("_conformance_proposalcheck", RULES_DIR / "bin" / "proposalcheck")
except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 -- item 7 reports this itself if it happens
    PC = None


def _mandatory_pending():
    """rulecheck.mandatory_pending (S-09) when this checkout's rulecheck has it, else None."""
    try:
        module = _load("_conformance_rulecheck", RULES_DIR / "bin" / "rulecheck")
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
        return None
    fn = getattr(module, "mandatory_pending", None)
    return fn if callable(fn) else None


MANDATORY_PENDING = _mandatory_pending()


def shown(value) -> str:
    return W.shown(value)


class Result(NamedTuple):
    # A NamedTuple, not a dataclass: @dataclass looks its module up in
    # sys.modules, and this file is loaded by path (no .py), where it is not.
    state: str
    why: str = ""
    fix: str = ""


def holds(why: str = "") -> Result:
    return Result(HOLDS, why, "")


def not_hold(why: str, fix: str) -> Result:
    return Result(NOT, why, fix)


def waiting(why: str, fix: str = "") -> Result:
    return Result(WAITING, why, fix)


class CouldNotRun(Exception):
    pass


def _env() -> dict:
    return dict(os.environ, GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS="0", PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE="1")


def run(cmd: list, input: str | None = None) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess | str:
    """The finished process, or a sentence saying why it did not finish."""
    feed = {"input": input} if input is not None else {"stdin": subprocess.DEVNULL}
    try:
        return subprocess.run([str(c) for c in cmd], capture_output=True, text=True, errors="replace",
                              check=False, timeout=TIMEOUT, env=_env(), **feed)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        return f"{Path(str(cmd[1] if cmd[0] == sys.executable else cmd[0])).name} did not finish in {TIMEOUT} s"
    except OSError as exc:
        return f"{Path(str(cmd[0])).name} could not be started ({type(exc).__name__})"


_INPROCESS_LOCK = threading.Lock()


def run_inprocess(fn, argv: list) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
    """Call an in-repo CLI's main(argv) in this process and return a
    CompletedProcess exactly as running it as a subprocess would have --
    same stdout, stderr and returncode -- so callers need not care which one
    ran. This is O-09 (31/F-01): bin/warmup and bin/proposalcheck are this
    repo's own scripts; importing and calling them skips a fresh interpreter
    start and a full re-compile of every module they load.

    Locked: contextlib.redirect_stdout/stderr swap the process-global
    sys.stdout/sys.stderr, not a thread-local -- with the checks now running
    concurrently (O-09), two callers at once would each capture (and worse,
    on __exit__ clobber) the other's output. Only the two checks that call
    this (7 and 12) ever contend for it; the other ten's git subprocesses
    are unaffected."""
    out, err = io.StringIO(), io.StringIO()
    argv = [str(a) for a in argv]
    with _INPROCESS_LOCK, contextlib.redirect_stdout(out), contextlib.redirect_stderr(err):
        try:
            code = fn(argv)
        except SystemExit as exc:
            code = exc.code if isinstance(exc.code, int) else (1 if exc.code else 0)
        except KeyboardInterrupt:
            raise
        except BaseException:
            # A crashed subprocess would have produced a non-zero returncode
            # and the traceback on stderr, and the check that called it just
            # reported "does not hold" -- a bad file in one project failed
            # one check. Letting the exception propagate here instead would
            # take the whole thread pool down with it: one bad ledger failing
            # all twelve checks instead of just item 7 or 12.
            traceback.print_exc(file=err)
            code = 1
    return subprocess.CompletedProcess(args=argv, returncode=code, stdout=out.getvalue(), stderr=err.getvalue())


def _joined(values: list, cap: int = FIRST) -> str:
    head = ", ".join(str(v) for v in values[:cap])
    return head + (f" (+{len(values) - cap} more)" if len(values) > cap else "")


def _first_line(text: str) -> str:
    return next((l.strip() for l in text.splitlines() if l.strip()), "")


class Context:
    def __init__(self, root: Path):
        self.root = Path(root)
        self._ledgers: list | None = None
        self._ledgers_lock = threading.Lock()

    def git(self, *args: str, input: str | None = None) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess | str:
        return run(["git", "-c", "core.fsmonitor=false", "-C", self.root, *args], input=input)

    def git_out(self, *args: str, input: str | None = None) -> str | None:
        r = self.git(*args, input=input)
        return r.stdout if isinstance(r, subprocess.CompletedProcess) and r.returncode == 0 else None

    def rel(self, path: Path) -> str:
        try:
            return str(Path(path).relative_to(self.root))
        except ValueError:
            return str(path)

    def ledgers(self) -> list[tuple[Path, dict | None, str | None]]:
        """(path, the ledger or None, why it could not be read or None), in find() order.
        Locked: the checks that call this run concurrently (O-09), and a bare
        `if self._ledgers is None` race would have two threads compute -- and
        one overwrite the other's -- the same list."""
        with self._ledgers_lock:
            if self._ledgers is None:
                self._ledgers = self._load_ledgers()
            return self._ledgers

    def _load_ledgers(self) -> list[tuple[Path, dict | None, str | None]]:
        out = []
        for p in L.find(self.root):
            try:
                data = L.load(p)
            except (OSError, ValueError):
                out.append((p, None, "is not valid JSON"))
                continue
            if not isinstance(data, dict):
                out.append((p, None, "is not a JSON object"))
                continue
            out.append((p, data, None))
        return out

    def declared(self) -> dict:
        try:
            return P.declared(self.root)
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            return {}


def common_dir(path: Path) -> Path | None:
    r = run(["git", "-C", path, "rev-parse", "--git-common-dir"])
    if not isinstance(r, subprocess.CompletedProcess) or r.returncode != 0 or not r.stdout.strip():
        return None
    p = Path(r.stdout.strip())
    return (p if p.is_absolute() else Path(path) / p).resolve()


def toplevel(project: Path) -> Path | None:
    if not Path(project).is_dir():
        return None
    r = run(["git", "-C", project, "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"])
    if not isinstance(r, subprocess.CompletedProcess) or r.returncode != 0 or not r.stdout.strip():
        return None
    return Path(r.stdout.strip())


# --- 1 -----------------------------------------------------------------------

def is_rules_repository(root: Path) -> bool:
    if Path(root).resolve() == RULES_DIR.resolve():
        return True
    mine, theirs = common_dir(RULES_DIR), common_dir(root)
    return mine is not None and mine == theirs


def check_rules(ctx: Context) -> Result:
    root = ctx.root
    if is_rules_repository(root):
        return holds("this is the rules repository itself -- rulecheck: nothing to align against")
    align = f"{RULES_DIR}/bin/rulecheck --project {root} --align"
    if not (root / STAMP).is_file():
        return not_hold(f"{STAMP} is absent -- the project has never recorded which rules it follows",
                        f"read common-rules' CHANGELOG.md, implement every Standard change entry, then run "
                        f"{align} and commit the stamp")
    if not stamp_committed(ctx):
        return not_hold(f"{STAMP} is not committed -- untracked, or it differs from HEAD -- so the project's "
                        f"record does not say which rules it follows",
                        f"commit {STAMP}; if it is behind, implement what rulecheck names first, then {align}")
    fn = MANDATORY_PENDING
    if fn is None:
        return waiting("RULES' bin/rulecheck has no mandatory_pending() (S-09), so a mandatory Standard change "
                       "cannot be told from information",
                       "nothing to do in the project; update common-rules")
    # rulecheck runs git in-process, against the rules repo: the same promise
    # the child processes get, never an index refresh.
    os.environ["GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS"] = "0"
    try:
        p = fn(root)
    except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001
        return not_hold(f"rulecheck's mandatory_pending() failed ({type(exc).__name__})",
                        f"run {RULES_DIR}/bin/rulecheck --project {root} --mandatory and fix what it names")
    entries = tuple(p.entries or ())
    headings = _joined([e.heading for e in entries], 3)
    implement = (f"implement each entry and record it in the ledger (skills/standard section 3), then run "
                 f"{align} and commit the stamp")
    if p.state == "aligned":
        return holds(f"stamp aligned with common-rules {p.current}")
    if p.state == "ahead":
        return holds(f"stamp {p.stamp} is ahead of this rules checkout -- update common-rules")
    if p.state == "behind" and not entries:
        return holds(f"stamp {p.stamp} is behind {p.current} on information only -- no mandatory Standard "
                     f"change pending")
    if p.state == "behind":
        return not_hold(f"{len(entries)} mandatory Standard change(s) pending since the stamp: {headings}", implement)
    if p.state == "unresolvable":
        listed = (f"; every mandatory Standard change counts ({len(entries)}): {headings}" if entries
                  else "; the rules CHANGELOG carries no mandatory Standard change")
        return not_hold(f"the stamp names no commit in common-rules{listed}", implement)
    if p.state == "no stamp":
        return not_hold(f"{STAMP} is absent", implement)
    if p.state == "could not check":
        return not_hold(f"rulecheck could not check the stamp: {p.why}",
                        f"run {RULES_DIR}/bin/rulecheck --project {root} --mandatory and fix what it names")
    if p.state == "not adopting":
        return not_hold("rulecheck does not count the project as adopting the rules, though it has a stamp",
                        f"run {RULES_DIR}/bin/rulecheck --project {root} and fix what it names")
    return not_hold(f"rulecheck answered a state bin/conformance does not know: {p.state!r}",
                    "nothing the project can do; common-rules must teach bin/conformance this state")


def stamp_committed(ctx: Context) -> bool:
    """The stamp is tracked, and the file on disk is what HEAD holds. Compared
    by content, not `git diff`, which reads stale stat data as a change -- and
    as bytes: text-mode output turns \\r\\n into \\n, so a stamp committed as
    it is on disk read "not committed" (S-04 final review)."""
    if ctx.git_out("ls-files", "--error-unmatch", "--", STAMP) is None:
        return False
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(ctx.root), "-c", "core.fsmonitor=false", "cat-file", "blob",
                            f"HEAD:{STAMP}"], capture_output=True, check=False, timeout=TIMEOUT, env=_env(),
                           stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)
        on_disk = (ctx.root / STAMP).read_bytes()
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return False
    return r.returncode == 0 and r.stdout == on_disk


# --- 2 -----------------------------------------------------------------------

def check_migrated(ctx: Context) -> Result:
    ledgers = ctx.ledgers()
    migrate = f"{RULES_DIR}/bin/warmup --project {ctx.root} --migrate --dry-run --at <now>, then without --dry-run"
    if not ledgers:
        return not_hold("no ledger under docs/proposals (docs/proposals/NN-*.json with an items list)",
                        f"run {migrate}; a plan that is not a ledger yet becomes one with tracker import")
    bad, own_pages = [], 0
    # Proposal 22, T-02: the project's one page first, so it is always named.
    board_state, board_page = B.project_page_state(ctx.root)
    if board_state != "ok":
        bad.append(f"page {ctx.rel(board_page)} is {board_state}")
    for path, data, err in ledgers:
        rel = ctx.rel(path)
        if err:
            bad.append(f"{rel} {err}")
            continue
        problems = L.validate(data)
        if problems:
            bad.append(f"{rel}: {len(problems)} problem(s), first: {problems[0]}")
            continue
        if not L.own_tracker(data):
            continue
        page = R.default_out(path)
        fresh = R.freshness(path, page)
        if fresh != "ok":
            bad.append(f"page {ctx.rel(page)} is {fresh}")
        else:
            own_pages += 1
    if bad:
        return not_hold("; ".join(bad[:3]) + (f" (+{len(bad) - 3} more)" if len(bad) > 3 else ""),
                        f"fix what {RULES_DIR}/bin/tracker validate names, then run {RULES_DIR}/bin/tracker "
                        f"board --project {ctx.root}, and {RULES_DIR}/bin/tracker render <ledger> for a ledger "
                        f"that records its own tracker (the pre-commit hook does both on commit)")
    return holds(f"{len(ledgers)} ledger(s), each valid, the project page matching"
                 + (f", {own_pages} own tracker page(s) matching" if own_pages else ""))


# --- 3 -----------------------------------------------------------------------

def check_declared(ctx: Context) -> Result:
    if not (ctx.root / P.FILE).is_file():
        return not_hold(f"{P.FILE} is absent",
                        f"declare read_order, safety_rules, gates.quick and gates.merge in {P.FILE}")
    problems = P.problems(ctx.root)
    if problems:
        return not_hold(f"{P.FILE} has {len(problems)} problem(s), first: {problems[0]}",
                        f"fix {P.FILE}; {RULES_DIR}/bin/warmup --check names every problem")
    own = ctx.declared()
    missing = [k for k in ("read_order", "safety_rules") if k not in own]
    missing += [f"gates.{g}" for g in ("quick", "merge") if g not in own.get("gates", {})]
    if missing:
        return not_hold(f"{P.FILE} does not declare {', '.join(missing)}",
                        f"declare {', '.join(missing)} in {P.FILE}")
    return holds("declares read_order, safety_rules, gates.quick and gates.merge")


# --- 4 -----------------------------------------------------------------------

@functools.lru_cache(maxsize=None)
def _rules_common_dir() -> Path | None:
    return common_dir(RULES_DIR)


def is_rules_checkout(path: Path) -> bool:
    """`path` is this common-rules checkout, or the top of a checkout sharing its
    git common directory (the main checkout, or another worktree of it)."""
    try:
        real = Path(path).resolve()
    except (OSError, RuntimeError, ValueError):
        return False
    if real == RULES_DIR.resolve():
        return True
    if not real.is_dir():
        return False
    top = toplevel(real)
    mine = _rules_common_dir()
    return top is not None and top.resolve() == real and mine is not None and common_dir(real) == mine


def derecord_hook_template() -> str | None:
    """The hook body bin/derecord writes, read from derecord itself: its
    `<<'HOOKBODY'` heredoc, with __RULES_DIR__ still in it."""
    text = (RULES_DIR / "bin" / "derecord").read_text(errors="replace")
    m = re.search(r"<<'HOOKBODY'\n(.*?\n)HOOKBODY\n", text, re.S)
    return m.group(1) if m else None


def hook_is_derecords(path: Path, template: str) -> bool:
    """The hook is byte for byte what this derecord installs: its template with
    a rules checkout substituted, under the marker derecord computes. A
    self-consistent marker is only derecord's "may I overwrite" test -- a hook
    edited by hand, from an older derecord, or naming a copy of the rules
    passes that and fails this."""
    text = path.read_bytes().decode("utf-8", errors="replace")
    m = re.search(r'^RULES="([^"\n]*)"$', text, re.M)
    if not m or not is_rules_checkout(Path(m.group(1))):
        return False
    body = template.replace("__RULES_DIR__", m.group(1))
    return text == f"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# derecord-body-sha256: {hashlib.sha256(body.encode()).hexdigest()}\n{body}"


def derecord_ignore_lines() -> list[str]:
    text = (RULES_DIR / "bin" / "derecord").read_text(errors="replace")
    m = re.search(r"^IGNORE_LINES=\(\n(.*?)^\)", text, re.M | re.S)
    return re.findall(r'"([^"\n]+)"', m.group(1)) if m else []


def ignored_by_gitignore(ctx: Context, line: str) -> bool:
    """git ignores the line's path -- as written, or as a directory -- by a
    .gitignore file inside the project, with a pattern that is not a negation.
    .git/info/exclude and a personal core.excludesFile are this machine's, not
    the project's: a clone would track the file, so neither counts. -z, so a
    source or path with a colon or quoting cannot be misread -- and git
    refuses -z without --stdin ("-z only makes sense with --stdin", exit 128),
    so the path goes in on stdin, NUL-terminated, never as an argument."""
    for probe in dict.fromkeys((line, line.rstrip("/") + "/")):
        out = ctx.git_out("check-ignore", "-v", "-z", "--stdin", "--no-index", input=probe + "\0")
        fields = (out or "").split("\0")
        if len(fields) < 4:
            continue
        source, pattern = fields[0], fields[2]
        where = PurePosixPath(source)
        if (source and not where.is_absolute() and ".." not in where.parts and where.name == ".gitignore"
                and not where.parts[0] == ".git" and not pattern.startswith("!")):
            return True
    return False


def check_installed(ctx: Context) -> Result:
    root = ctx.root
    problems: list[str] = []
    template = derecord_hook_template()
    if template is None:
        return waiting("bin/derecord no longer writes its hook from a <<'HOOKBODY' heredoc where conformance "
                       "reads it", "nothing to do in the project; common-rules must reconcile the two")
    derecord = f"{RULES_DIR}/bin/derecord {root}"
    fixes = [f"run {derecord}"]

    hooks_dir = ctx.git_out("rev-parse", "--git-path", "hooks")
    hook = None
    if hooks_dir and hooks_dir.strip():
        hook = Path(hooks_dir.strip())
        hook = (hook if hook.is_absolute() else root / hook) / "pre-commit"
    if hook is None or not hook.is_file():
        problems.append("no git pre-commit hook")
    elif not hook_is_derecords(hook, template):
        problems.append("the git pre-commit hook is not the one this common-rules' bin/derecord installs "
                        "(edited by hand, from an older derecord, or naming another rules checkout)")

    if root.resolve() == RULES_DIR.resolve():
        hooks_path = root / "hooks" / "hooks.json"
        try:
            plugin_hooks = json.loads(hooks_path.read_text()).get("hooks", {})
        except (OSError, ValueError, AttributeError):
            plugin_hooks = {}
            problems.append("hooks/hooks.json is not valid plugin hook JSON")
        for event, name in SETTINGS_HOOKS:
            commands = [h.get("command") for e in (plugin_hooks.get(event) or []) if isinstance(e, dict)
                        for h in (e.get("hooks") or []) if isinstance(h, dict)]
            expected = f'"${{CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}}/hooks/{name}"'
            if expected not in commands:
                problems.append(f"no portable {event} hook running common-rules' hooks/{name}")
    else:
        settings_path = root / ".claude" / "settings.json"
        settings = None
        if not settings_path.is_file():
            problems.append("no .claude/settings.json")
        else:
            try:
                settings = json.loads(settings_path.read_text())
            except (OSError, ValueError):
                problems.append(".claude/settings.json is not valid JSON")
        if settings is not None:
            hooks = settings.get("hooks") if isinstance(settings, dict) else None
            hooks = hooks if isinstance(hooks, dict) else {}
            for event, name in SETTINGS_HOOKS:
                commands = [h.get("command") for e in (hooks.get(event) or []) if isinstance(e, dict)
                            for h in (e.get("hooks") or []) if isinstance(h, dict)]
                ok = False
                for c in commands:
                    if not isinstance(c, str) or not Path(c.strip()).is_absolute():
                        continue
                    try:
                        real = Path(c.strip()).resolve()
                    except (OSError, RuntimeError, ValueError):
                        continue
                    # Resolved: RULES_DIR/hooks/<name>, or hooks/<name> in a checkout
                    # sharing its git common directory. A copied hooks/ is not ours.
                    if (real.name == name and real.parent.name == "hooks" and real.is_file()
                            and is_rules_checkout(real.parent.parent)):
                        ok = True
                        break
                if not ok:
                    problems.append(f"no {event} hook running common-rules' hooks/{name}")

    lines = derecord_ignore_lines()
    if not lines:
        return waiting("bin/derecord no longer declares IGNORE_LINES where conformance reads them",
                       "nothing to do in the project; common-rules must reconcile the two")
    # Always git's answer: a verbatim line can be undone by a later `!line`.
    missing = [l for l in lines if not ignored_by_gitignore(ctx, l)]
    if missing:
        problems.append(f".gitignore does not ignore {_joined(missing)}")

    tracked: list[str] = []
    for line in lines:
        out = ctx.git_out("ls-files", "--", line.rstrip("/"))
        tracked += [t for t in (out or "").splitlines() if t]
    tracked = sorted(set(tracked))
    if tracked:
        problems.append(f"Ruflo runtime file(s) tracked: {_joined(tracked)}")
        fixes.append("untrack them, after asking the sponsor (the files stay on disk): git rm -r --cached "
                     + " ".join(tracked[:FIRST]))
    if problems:
        return not_hold("; ".join(problems), "; ".join(fixes))
    return holds("derecord's pre-commit hook, checkpoint and agent hooks, and Ruflo ignore lines are in place")


# --- 5 -----------------------------------------------------------------------

def lead_prompts(root: Path) -> list[Path]:
    docs = root / "docs"
    found = []
    if not docs.is_dir():
        return found
    for dirpath, dirnames, filenames in os.walk(docs, followlinks=False):
        depth = len(Path(dirpath).relative_to(docs).parts)
        dirnames[:] = [d for d in dirnames if d != "superseded" and depth < 2]
        for name in filenames:
            p = Path(dirpath) / name
            if (re.fullmatch(r".*lead-prompt.*\.md", name) and p.is_file() and not p.is_symlink()
                    and P.inside(root, str(p.relative_to(root)))):
                found.append(p)
    return sorted(found)


def check_lead_prompt(ctx: Context) -> Result:
    prompts = lead_prompts(ctx.root)
    template = f"{RULES_DIR}/templates/lead-prompt.md"
    if not prompts:
        return not_hold("no lead prompt under docs/ (docs/handovers/*lead-prompt*.md, or *lead-prompt*.md up to "
                        "three levels down)",
                        f"regenerate it from {template}; {RULES_DIR}/bin/derecord seeds docs/handovers/lead-prompt.md")
    try:
        carries = LEAD_MARKER in Path(LEAD_TEMPLATE).read_text(errors="replace")
    except OSError:
        carries = False
    if not carries:
        return waiting(f"RULES' templates/lead-prompt.md does not carry {LEAD_MARKER} yet (S-06), so a prompt "
                       f"regenerated from it cannot be told from an older one",
                       "nothing to do in the project yet; this re-measures once S-06 is merged")
    missing = [ctx.rel(p) for p in prompts if LEAD_MARKER not in p.read_text(errors="replace")]
    if missing:
        return not_hold(f"{_joined(missing, 3)} does not carry {LEAD_MARKER} -- not regenerated from the template",
                        f"regenerate it from {template}, carry the project's own rules into its sections, and keep "
                        f"the old prompt under docs/handovers/superseded/")
    return holds(f"{len(prompts)} lead prompt(s), each from the template")


# --- 6 -----------------------------------------------------------------------

def owned(row: dict) -> bool:
    """`owner` is sponsor, lead or session:<name> -- tools/tracker/ledger.py's OWNER."""
    return isinstance(row.get("owner"), str) and bool(L.OWNER.match(row["owner"]))


def tagged(row: dict) -> bool:
    """The tag is `[ruflo · <tier> · <model>]`, and exactly the row's own when it
    names a tier and a model."""
    tag = row.get("tag")
    if not isinstance(tag, str) or not TAG.fullmatch(tag):
        return False
    tier, model = row.get("tier"), row.get("model")
    if isinstance(tier, str) and tier and isinstance(model, str) and model:
        return tag == f"[ruflo · {tier} · {model}]"
    return True


def _request_problems(data: dict) -> list[str]:
    without = {k: v for k, v in data.items() if k != "requests"}
    other = set(L.validate(without))
    return [p for p in L.validate(data) if p not in other]


def check_language(ctx: Context) -> Result:
    ledgers = ctx.ledgers()
    if not ledgers:
        return not_hold("no ledger to speak it", "migrate first (item 2)")
    parts = []
    for path, data, err in ledgers:
        rel = ctx.rel(path)
        if err:
            parts.append(f"{rel} {err}")
            continue
        rows = [i for i in (data.get("items") or []) if isinstance(i, dict)]
        asks = [a for a in (data.get("asks") or []) if isinstance(a, dict)]

        def ident(x, n, kind):
            return x.get("id") if x.get("id") else f"{kind}[{n}]"

        blocked = [ident(i, n, "items") for n, i in enumerate(rows) if i.get("status") == "blocked" and not owned(i)]
        open_asks = [ident(a, n, "asks") for n, a in enumerate(asks)
                     if a.get("state") == "open" and a.get("kind") == "decision" and not owned(a)]
        untagged = [ident(i, n, "items") for n, i in enumerate(rows) if not tagged(i)]
        tables = [k for k in ("tiers", "model_routing") if k in data]
        requests = _request_problems(data)
        mine = []
        if blocked:
            mine.append(f"blocked without a valid owner: {_joined(blocked)}")
        if open_asks:
            mine.append(f"open decision asks without a valid owner: {_joined(open_asks)}")
        if untagged:
            mine.append(f"rows whose tag is not [ruflo · <tier> · <model>] for their own tier and model: "
                        f"{_joined(untagged)}")
        if len(tables) != 1:
            mine.append("two routing tables, tiers and model_routing" if tables else
                        "no routing table (tiers or model_routing)")
        if requests:
            mine.append(f"requests: {_joined(requests, 3)}")
        if mine:
            parts.append(f"{rel}: " + "; ".join(mine))
    if parts:
        return not_hold(" | ".join(parts),
                        "give every blocked row and open decision ask an owner (sponsor, lead or session:<name>), "
                        "every row its [ruflo · tier · model] tag, keep exactly one routing table, and fix the "
                        "requests tracker validate names")
    return holds(f"{len(ledgers)} ledger(s): owners, tags, one routing table, requests valid")


# --- 7 -----------------------------------------------------------------------

def check_proposals(ctx: Context) -> Result:
    fix = f"fix what {RULES_DIR}/bin/proposalcheck --project {ctx.root} names; create proposals with bin/new-proposal"
    if PC is None:
        return not_hold("bin/proposalcheck could not be loaded", fix)
    r = run_inprocess(PC.main, ["--project", ctx.root])
    if isinstance(r, str):
        return not_hold(r, fix)
    if r.returncode == 0:
        return holds(_first_line(r.stdout.splitlines()[-1] if r.stdout.strip() else ""))
    if r.returncode == 2:
        return not_hold("no docs/proposals/ -- proposalcheck has nothing to check",
                        f"create proposals with {RULES_DIR}/bin/new-proposal")
    last = [l for l in r.stdout.splitlines() if l.strip()]
    count = last[-1].strip() if last else f"exit {r.returncode}"
    return not_hold(f"proposalcheck: {_first_line(r.stdout) or _first_line(r.stderr)} ({count})", fix)


# --- 8 -----------------------------------------------------------------------

def check_duplicates(ctx: Context) -> Result:
    if "plan_page" in ctx.declared():
        return holds("declares plan_page -- its page is generated, not hand-kept")
    twins = []
    for path, _data, _err in ctx.ledgers():
        md = path.with_suffix(".md")
        if not (md.exists() or md.is_symlink()):
            continue
        text = ""
        if md.is_file() and not md.is_symlink() and P.inside(ctx.root, ctx.rel(md)):
            text = md.read_text(errors="replace")
        if len(text.splitlines()) <= 5 and path.name in text:
            continue
        twins.append(ctx.rel(md))
    if twins:
        return not_hold(f"{_joined(twins)} mirror(s) a ledger by hand: more than 5 lines, or not naming the ledger file",
                        "retire each to a pointer of at most 5 lines naming its ledger file, or declare plan_page")
    return holds("no hand-kept twin of a ledger")


# --- 9 -----------------------------------------------------------------------

def _log_date(row: dict, *, matches) -> "datetime.date | None":
    """The `at` of the last log entry `matches` accepts, or None. A malformed
    or missing `at` is the same as no entry."""
    found = None
    for entry in (row.get("log") or []):
        if not isinstance(entry, dict) or not matches(entry):
            continue
        at = entry.get("at")
        if not isinstance(at, str):
            continue
        try:
            found = datetime.date.fromisoformat(at[:10])
        except ValueError:
            continue
    return found


def _done_log_date(row: dict) -> "datetime.date | None":
    """The date `row` closed, or None when nothing says. `tracker set
    --status done` (and `apply-staged`, which shares its code) stamps the log
    entry's own `status` key with the new status independently of `event`
    (RF-01) -- `event` is free text a lead writes with `--event`, and 35 of
    92 done items in the real ledgers already close with one that is not
    literally "done", so a status-keyed entry is preferred when there is
    one. Older rows never wrote `status`: the last entry whose `event` is
    exactly "done" (case-insensitive) is the fallback, as before this
    module read `status` at all."""
    by_status = _log_date(row, matches=lambda e: str(e.get("status") or "").strip().lower() == "done")
    if by_status is not None:
        return by_status
    return _log_date(row, matches=lambda e: str(e.get("event") or "").strip().lower() == "done")


def _swarm_db(ctx: Context) -> Path | None:
    """DIR's own .swarm/memory.db, else its main checkout's -- the database
    lives only in the main checkout, never in a worktree (mirrors bin/
    ruflo-item's own ruflo_cwd_for()). None when neither is a non-empty file."""
    bases = [ctx.root]
    common = common_dir(ctx.root)
    if common is not None and common.name == ".git" and common.parent not in bases:
        bases.append(common.parent)
    for base in bases:
        db = base / ".swarm" / "memory.db"
        try:
            if db.is_file() and db.stat().st_size > 0:
                return db
        except OSError:
            continue
    return None


def _memory_keys(db: Path, namespace: str) -> set[str] | None:
    """Every key recorded under `namespace` in `db`'s memory_entries table --
    read READ-ONLY (`file:...?mode=ro`), never through the Ruflo CLI, which
    auto-starts its daemon. None when `db` is not a readable sqlite database
    with that table."""
    try:
        conn = sqlite3.connect(f"file:{db.as_posix()}?mode=ro", uri=True, timeout=5)
    except sqlite3.Error:
        return None
    try:
        rows = conn.execute("SELECT key FROM memory_entries WHERE namespace = ?", (namespace,)).fetchall()
    except sqlite3.Error:
        return None
    finally:
        conn.close()
    return {r[0] for r in rows}


def check_ruflo(ctx: Context) -> Result:
    ledgers = [(p, d) for p, d, err in ctx.ledgers() if not err]
    if not ledgers:
        return not_hold("no ledger under docs/proposals -- there is nothing to check Ruflo evidence against",
                        f"run {RULES_DIR}/bin/warmup --project {ctx.root} --migrate first")
    for path, data in ledgers:
        if not L.switch_on(data, "ruflo"):
            sw = data["switches"]["ruflo"]
            return holds(f"switched off by {sw.get('by')} at {sw.get('at')} ({ctx.rel(path)})")

    db = _swarm_db(ctx)
    if db is None:
        return not_hold(".swarm/memory.db is absent or empty -- no Ruflo memory to check",
                        f"run {RULES_DIR}/bin/ruflo-item start | done around every item; it writes "
                        f".swarm/memory.db in the main checkout")

    ns = RU.namespace_for(ctx.root)
    keys = _memory_keys(db, ns)
    if keys is None:
        return not_hold(f"{ctx.rel(db)} is not a readable Ruflo memory database (no memory_entries table)",
                        f"run {RULES_DIR}/bin/ruflo-item start | done around every item")

    # Every closed id needing a record, and how many closed items predate the
    # cutoff and were therefore never checked (some of those may still have a
    # record from before the cutoff mattered -- that's tallied too, at no
    # extra cost, since `keys` is already in hand). An id in two ledgers (a
    # collision) needs only one record either way -- Ruflo memory is keyed by
    # id alone, so a set naturally covers both.
    required, before_cutoff, before_cutoff_with_record = set(), 0, 0
    for path, data in ledgers:
        for row in (data.get("items") or []):
            if not isinstance(row, dict) or row.get("status") != "done" or not row.get("id"):
                continue
            date = _done_log_date(row)
            if date is not None and date >= RUFLO_CUTOFF:
                required.add(row["id"])
            else:
                before_cutoff += 1
                if f"item:{row['id']}:done" in keys:
                    before_cutoff_with_record += 1

    missing = sorted(i for i in required if f"item:{i}:start" not in keys or f"item:{i}:done" not in keys)
    note = (f" -- {before_cutoff} item(s) closed before {RUFLO_CUTOFF.isoformat()} not checked, of which "
           f"{before_cutoff_with_record} have records" if before_cutoff else "")
    if missing:
        return not_hold(f"{_joined(missing)} closed on or after {RUFLO_CUTOFF.isoformat()} without both "
                        f"item:<ID>:start and item:<ID>:done in Ruflo memory (namespace {shown(ns)}){note}",
                        f"run {RULES_DIR}/bin/ruflo-item start <ID> \"...\" and done <ID> \"...\" for each "
                        f"missing id, then re-run conformance")
    return holds(f"{len(required)} item(s) closed on or after {RUFLO_CUTOFF.isoformat()} all have Ruflo "
                f"start and done records (namespace {shown(ns)}){note}")


# --- 10 ----------------------------------------------------------------------

def check_publishing(ctx: Context) -> Result:
    tracker = ctx.root / "docs" / "proposals" / "tracker"
    sidecars = sorted(tracker.glob("*.published.json")) if tracker.is_dir() else []
    if not sidecars:
        return holds("no published page recorded")
    plan_page = "plan_page" in ctx.declared()
    bad, leftover = [], False
    for sidecar in sidecars:
        if sidecar.name == B.PUBLISHED_NAME:
            # Proposal 22, T-03: the project's one page, read as bin/warmup reads it.
            _pub, problem, _label = W.project_publish_state(
                ctx.root, B.default_out(ctx.root), [d for _p, d, err in ctx.ledgers() if not err])
            if problem:
                bad.append(problem)
            continue
        stem = sidecar.name[:-len(".published.json")]
        ledger = ctx.root / "docs" / "proposals" / f"{stem}.json"
        data = None
        if ledger.is_file():
            try:
                data = L.load(ledger)
            except (OSError, ValueError):
                data = None
        if isinstance(data, dict):
            if not (L.own_tracker(data) or plan_page or W.records_a_page(sidecar)):
                # T-03: nothing renders or publishes this page any more.
                leftover = True
                bad.append(f"{ctx.rel(sidecar)} records a page for {stem}.json, which records no tracker of its "
                           f"own -- the project publishes one page, and that record is "
                           f"docs/proposals/tracker/{B.PUBLISHED_NAME}")
                continue
            _pub, problem, _label = W.publish_state(ctx.root, ledger, data, R.default_out(ledger))
            if problem:
                bad.append(problem)
            continue
        rel = ctx.rel(sidecar)
        if sidecar.is_symlink() or not P.inside(ctx.root, rel):
            bad.append(f"{rel} is a symlink or resolves outside the project -- not read")
            continue
        try:
            record = json.loads(sidecar.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
        except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError, ValueError, RecursionError) as exc:
            bad.append(f"{rel} is malformed ({type(exc).__name__})")
            continue
        problems = R.published_problems(record)
        if problems:
            bad.append(f"{rel} is malformed: {'; '.join(problems)}")
    if bad:
        record = (f"republish the page and record it with {RULES_DIR}/bin/tracker published --project "
                  f"{ctx.root} --url <url>, then commit the sidecar on its own (a ledger with a tracker of "
                  f"its own, or a declared plan_page: tracker published <ledger> --url <url> [--page <path>])")
        if leftover:
            record = "git rm the per-ledger sidecar named above, then " + record
        return not_hold("; ".join(bad[:3]) + (f" (+{len(bad) - 3} more)" if len(bad) > 3 else ""), record)
    return holds(f"{len(sidecars)} publish record(s), each well-formed")


# --- 11 ----------------------------------------------------------------------

def _block(text: str) -> str | None:
    if M.BEGIN not in text or M.END not in text:
        return None
    a = text.index(M.BEGIN)
    end = text.find(M.END, a)
    return text[a:end + len(M.END)] if end >= 0 else None


def check_pointer(ctx: Context) -> Result:
    migrate = f"{RULES_DIR}/bin/warmup --project {ctx.root} --migrate"
    commit = "show the sponsor the diff, then commit the pointer block (/standard authorizes that and nothing else in CLAUDE.md)"
    cm = ctx.root / "CLAUDE.md"
    if cm.is_symlink() or (cm.exists() and not P.inside(ctx.root, "CLAUDE.md")):
        # The policy of items 5 and 10: a symlink, or a path outside the project, is never read.
        return not_hold("CLAUDE.md is a symlink, or resolves outside the project -- not read",
                        f"make CLAUDE.md a regular file in the project, run {migrate}; {commit}")
    if not cm.is_file():
        return not_hold("no CLAUDE.md", f"run {migrate}; it writes the warm-up pointer block; {commit}")
    block = _block(cm.read_text(errors="replace"))
    if block is None:
        return not_hold(f"CLAUDE.md has no {M.BEGIN} block", f"run {migrate}; {commit}")
    if block != M.pointer_block(P.load(ctx.root)["read_order"]):
        return not_hold("the warm-up block in CLAUDE.md is not the one migrate writes for the declared read order",
                        f"run {migrate} to rewrite it; {commit}")
    if ctx.git_out("ls-files", "--error-unmatch", "--", "CLAUDE.md") is None:
        return not_hold("CLAUDE.md is not committed", commit)
    # `git diff HEAD`, as plumbing: porcelain `git diff` refreshes .git/index
    # and writes it back when the stat data is stale (a fresh clone or copy),
    # and GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0 does not stop it -- measured by
    # tests/test_conformance.py's snapshot. diff-index never writes the index.
    # A stat-only difference it reports is harmless: the block comparison
    # below decides.
    diff = ctx.git_out("diff-index", "-p", "--no-ext-diff", "--no-textconv", "--no-color", "HEAD", "--", "CLAUDE.md")
    if diff is None:
        return not_hold("CLAUDE.md cannot be compared with HEAD", commit)
    if diff.strip():
        at_head = ctx.git_out("show", "--no-textconv", "HEAD:CLAUDE.md")
        if at_head is None or _block(at_head) != block:
            return not_hold("the warm-up block in CLAUDE.md has an uncommitted change", commit)
    return holds("CLAUDE.md carries the committed warm-up block for the declared read order")


# --- 12 ----------------------------------------------------------------------

def check_card(ctx: Context) -> Result:
    fix = f"run {RULES_DIR}/bin/warmup --project {ctx.root} --check and fix each problem it names"
    if W is None:
        return not_hold("bin/warmup could not be loaded", fix)
    r = run_inprocess(W.main, ["--project", ctx.root, "--check", "--no-recall"])
    if isinstance(r, str):
        return not_hold(r, fix)
    if r.returncode == 0:
        return holds("warmup --check: ready")
    lines = r.stdout.splitlines()
    summary = next((l.strip() for l in lines if l.startswith("warmup --check:")), f"exit {r.returncode}")
    at = next((i for i, l in enumerate(lines) if l.startswith("warmup --check:")), len(lines))
    first = next((l.strip()[2:] for l in lines[at + 1:] if l.strip().startswith("✗ ")), _first_line(r.stderr))
    return not_hold(f"{summary}" + (f", first: {first}" if first else ""), fix)


ITEMS = (
    (1, "Rules read and implemented", check_rules),
    (2, "Migrated", check_migrated),
    (3, "Declared", check_declared),
    (4, "Installed", check_installed),
    (5, "Lead prompt", check_lead_prompt),
    (6, "The ledger speaks the common language", check_language),
    (7, "Proposals", check_proposals),
    (8, "No hand-kept duplicates", check_duplicates),
    (9, "Ruflo around items", check_ruflo),
    (10, "Publishing recorded", check_publishing),
    (11, "CLAUDE.md pointer", check_pointer),
    (12, "Card", check_card),
)


def _run_check(ctx: "Context", check) -> "Result":
    try:
        return check(ctx)
    except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 -- one broken check must not hide the other eleven
        return not_hold(f"the check itself failed ({type(exc).__name__}: {exc})",
                        "report this to common-rules; the item cannot be shown to hold")


def measure(project: Path) -> dict:
    """The report, every string already escaped. Raises CouldNotRun.

    O-09 (31/F-01): the twelve checks only read the repo, so they run
    concurrently -- a thread pool, since each one is dominated by waiting on
    git or another subprocess, not by CPU. ThreadPoolExecutor.map returns
    results in the order its iterable was given, not completion order, so
    `items` comes out in ITEMS' declared 1..12 order regardless of which
    check finishes first: the card's text is identical run to run."""
    root = toplevel(Path(project))
    if root is None:
        raise CouldNotRun(f"{project} is not inside a git repository")
    ctx = Context(root)
    with ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=len(ITEMS)) as pool:
        results = list(pool.map(lambda t: _run_check(ctx, t[2]), ITEMS))
    items = [{"n": n, "name": name, "state": result.state, "why": shown(result.why), "fix": shown(result.fix)}
             for (n, name, _check), result in zip(ITEMS, results)]
    return {"project": shown(root), "hold": sum(1 for i in items if i["state"] == HOLDS),
            "total": TOTAL, "items": items}


def exit_code(data: dict) -> int:
    return 1 if any(i["state"] == NOT for i in data["items"]) else 0


def text(data: dict) -> str:
    out = [f"standard: {data['hold']} of {data['total']} hold"]
    for i in data["items"]:
        head = f"{i['n']} {i['name']}"
        if i["state"] == HOLDS:
            out.append(f"✓ {head}" + (f": {i['why']}" if i["why"] else ""))
        elif i["state"] == WAITING:
            out.append(f"… {head}: waiting on common-rules -- {i['why']}" + (f" -- {i['fix']}" if i["fix"] else ""))
        else:
            out.append(f"✗ {head}: {i['why']} -- fix: {i['fix']}")
    return "\n".join(out)


def main(argv) -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="conformance", description=__doc__.splitlines()[0])
    ap.add_argument("--project", type=Path, default=Path.cwd())
    ap.add_argument("--json", action="store_true", help="the report as JSON")
    args = ap.parse_args(argv)
    if W is None:
        print("conformance: bin/warmup could not be loaded -- could not run", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    try:
        data = measure(args.project)
    except CouldNotRun as exc:
        print(f"conformance: {shown(exc)} -- could not run", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    print(json.dumps(data, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False) if args.json else text(data))
    return exit_code(data)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
